we're here in the permian basin of west  texas where some say  this leaking well could be the sign of a  bigger problem under our feet  this chevron well was designed to inject  oil field wastewater  deep underground and it did so for  decades  it was plugged more than 25 years ago  but it started to leak the very  same toxic water it was designed to get  rid of  chevron sent crews to fix the leak and  find out what happened  but with other leaking wells and  pipelines on the same ranch  the landowner and others fear an  ecological disaster could be taking  place  i'm a fourth generation west texas  rancher  my great grandfather came out here in  1914 and we've been working cattle in  west texas  ever since the problem today is that we  have a chevron well  blowing out that was previously plugged  and abandoned we don't know why it's  blowing out  which is even more concerning than the  blowout itself we also have a number  other wells that are having various  casing issues pressure issues etc this  is a symptom of the problem this is not  the problem itself the problem is the  other hundreds of wells on this ranch  alone  extrapolating thousands of wells in the  permian tens of thousands  how many of those are going to collapse  even it's only one percent that's 100  wells  that are going to pollute aquifers and  destroy huge swaths of land  our water out here is at 53 feet so any  contaminants that get to the surface  are going to get in our water system and  we live in a very dry climate so if we  lose our water  we don't have a ranch people can't live  here cattle can't live here nothing can  live here  right now we are destroying this aquifer  and i legitimately don't know if it can  ever be fixed  like it is a very open question whether  any amount of money in the world  can make this right  what's happening here remains under  investigation but some fear it's the  sign of a wider scale problem  one where hundreds or even thousands of  old wells in the nation's most prolific  oil field  could also start leaking some say it  could be a  potential problem one that both industry  and regulators will have to deal with
 

